WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.260
This episode of Things You Missed is brought
to you by the new CZsWorld Merch Store.

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:13.070
Stick around to the end of this video to hear
about our free merch giveaway.

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:17.230
The biggest theme that I picked up on in The
Shape of Water, was actually not water, but

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:18.550
rather, time.

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:23.530
Right off from the opening scene, we are bombarded
with symbols related to time.

00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:28.470
Elisa’s life is constantly on a tight schedule,
she keeps an alarm clock and a watch on the

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:32.710
table next to her bed, just two of the many
clocks seen around her apartment.

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:36.550
She’s always on top of keeping track of
the date using her calendar.

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:41.190
The calendar also features quotes on the back
of each page, one of which compares the flow

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:43.910
of a river to the flow of time.

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:47.289
But despite all this, she is constantly late.

00:00:47.289 --> 00:00:51.390
Whenever she shows up to work, her co-worker
Zelda always has to rush her and pull her

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:54.660
up to the front of the timestamp line so she
can clock in on time.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:58.720
The timestamps, by the way, are another time
reference, because being on time seems to

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:01.730
be a huge deal at the company that they work
at.

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:06.140
There’s a huge clock on the outside of the
building and Director Guillermo Del Toro really

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:09.000
wants his audience to notice the time motif.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.890
But the question is: what does it mean?

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:16.510
One of the timers that Elisa has in her apartment
is a kitchen timer which is shaped like an

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:17.510
egg.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:20.950
Eggs may have a greater significance than
just being the food that Elisa feeds Amphibian

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:22.490
Man.

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:27.650
They don’t really give him a name in the
movie, so I’m just gonna call him…

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:29.820
Gabe…

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:31.990
Gapien.

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:36.509
But if you look at it in a broader, more symbolic
sense in terms of their relationship; Elisa

00:01:36.509 --> 00:01:42.250
offers her Eggs to Gabe, which could be seen
as a symbol of reproductive fertility, especially

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:45.760
given the weird stuff that goes down later
in the movie.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:50.240
Which takes us back to Elisa’s egg timer
because human fertility is on a rather strict

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:55.110
timer as well, which Elisa, who looks to be
in her 40s is coming up on.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:59.340
And like Dimitri’s timer, the one he attaches
to the building’s circuit panel, once time

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:01.229
is up, it’s lights out.

00:02:01.229 --> 00:02:03.469
Although, that could just be a reference to
death.

00:02:03.469 --> 00:02:07.030
We do see the first kill take place in that
scene as a result.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:11.060
There’s also a scene where Elisa is waiting
for the bus and sits next to a man holding

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:13.740
a birthday cake, a symbol for aging.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:17.760
Then Giles during the part where he’s feeling
rejected and unwanted, he says he feels he

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:22.220
was born too early or too late, which is most
likely a response to him being kicked out

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:25.490
of his favorite pie place because of his sexual
orientation.

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:28.990
He wishes he lived in an era where he could
be accepted for who he is.

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:32.780
That also plays a part in the reason he helps
Elisa, because he can relate to her in the

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:34.930
sense that most of the world doesn’t understand
him.

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:39.489
That’s not to say that Giles didn’t need
some convincing and a slap in the face to

00:02:39.489 --> 00:02:40.670
get him going though.

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:44.260
Did anyone notice Elisa checking her watch
before hitting him?

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:46.819
This could be her trying to break out her
shell.

00:02:46.819 --> 00:02:50.910
She is usually quiet, reserved… and as I
mentioned, always late.

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:55.599
But in order to save Gabe, she knows she has
to change to challenge authority and be spot

00:02:55.599 --> 00:02:57.960
on time in order for the heist to work.

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:01.120
We know this because they have to synchronize
their watches and there’s a four minute

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:02.750
window to success.

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:07.040
Elisa still does end up being late during
the heist, but she gets lucky when Dimitri

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:09.030
ends up being on their side.

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:13.830
When Elisa develops her next plan, to release
Gabe on October 10th, she is once again faced

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:15.270
with the time challenge.

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:19.530
Dimitri tells her she needs to release him
by then as a health concern, Strickland is

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:24.150
given until then to bring back the asset with
his life on the line, so he bears after the

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:27.380
fishnapper, leaving Elisa no time to spare
anyway.

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:31.510
It is only then that she finally makes it
to the docks on time and gets herself and

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:36.360
Gabe to safety, presumably also beating the
biological clock as she takes a dive with

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.140
Gabe, this movie’s so weird…

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:43.180
The use of color also seems to tie into the
time theme that Del Toro has woven into the

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:44.180
story.

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:47.510
This is explained when Giles presents his
artwork to the Jello company and they tell

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:51.720
him that they don’t want red jello anymore
because green is the color of the future,

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.849
which led me to assume that red represented
the past.

00:03:54.849 --> 00:03:59.140
We also see some yellows in this movie, so
maybe they represent the present, you can

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:01.310
let me know your own ideas in the comments.

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:04.900
I think one of the reasons I can get behind
the idea, is because we see Strickland’s

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:09.670
transition to becoming what the Cadillac dealer
refers to as a man of the future.

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:13.622
When we first see Strickland’s family, they
are all wearing yellow and everything in the

00:04:13.622 --> 00:04:18.090
house is yellow, but he really wants to get
ahead of the curve in order to impress the

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:22.750
company he works for, where literally everything
is green or teal, they are interchangeable

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:23.750
here.

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:27.400
He buys the teal car, and as he’s driving
home, he passes a group of people in another

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:28.400
Cadillac.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:29.850
They are all wearing yellow.

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:33.380
This is the visual metaphor of him pulling
out of the past into the future.

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:37.290
We know he’s always craving green, since
we see him carry around those green candies

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:38.760
he enjoys so much.

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:43.770
We know he’s unsatisfied with his yellow
wife and goes after Elisa, a green who is

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:46.250
much more innovative and forward thinking.

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:50.350
After he gets the car we start to see his
family transition to green, at least in their

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:52.889
wardrobe, but his wife stays the same.

00:04:52.889 --> 00:04:57.020
Elisa on the other hand may be painted as
one of the most future oriented characters

00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:01.370
because she is almost always seen in green
and wears a large emerald throughout the movie.

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:05.381
The only time we see her regress to red is
when she loses confidence and she’s seen

00:05:05.381 --> 00:05:06.750
crying in the locker room.

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:12.150
I also noticed that in Strickland's office
there are three phones, red, black and green.

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:15.760
I’d have to see the movie again to remember
who called on which phone, but I’m willing

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:18.790
to bet it plays into the color trends I’ve
been pointing to.

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:21.530
Giles is also another example I can use.

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:25.800
He’s going after the job to draw that Jello
advertisement as I mentioned, and while doing

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:29.370
so he has cravings for Key Lime Pie, which
is teal.

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:33.460
After getting rejected, he’s feeling down,
and reverts to ordering a red pie, it looked

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:35.030
like maybe cherry.

00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:39.240
But when it comes to the heist, he knows he
has to step up his game, and as he mentioned,

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:42.750
be brave like Elisa, so he wears green for
that.

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:47.259
I could probably go on and on, because every
shot in the movie is coded with these colors,

00:05:47.259 --> 00:05:49.039
so let me know what you noticed in the comments.

00:05:49.039 --> 00:05:51.690
It’s mentioned that green and teal are perceived
very similarly.

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:56.110
I would maybe call some of those shades, like
the soap in the bathroom for example, more

00:05:56.110 --> 00:05:58.700
of an aquamarine, because believe me.

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:00.580
I know that color.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:03.310
I KNOW that color.

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:04.310
I KNOW.

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:05.310
THAT.

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:06.310
COLOR.

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:10.410
I think aquamarine is an appropriate color
choice for such an aqua heavy movie.

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:14.370
Outside of the obvious, flood bursting out
the bathroom door stuff, there were some interesting

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:18.639
and poetic uses uses of water in the movie,
such as Strickland intentionally spilling

00:06:18.639 --> 00:06:21.460
his glass of water to get to Elisa to come
clean it up.

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:26.500
In doing so, he takes advantage of Elisa’s
love for the aquatic to try to seduce her.

00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:31.470
As we know, it doesn’t work, and Stickland
is made an example of by another one of Elisa’s

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:35.620
calendar quotes: “Life is but the shipwreck
of our plans.”

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:40.111
He is much like the Greek mythological figure,
Tantalus, who Giles references earlier in

00:06:40.111 --> 00:06:41.111
the movie.

00:06:41.111 --> 00:06:45.660
He was made to stand in a pool of water beneath
a fruit tree with low branches, with the fruit

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:50.710
ever eluding his grasp, and the water always
receding before he could take a drink.

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:54.690
When Dimitri first meets the Russian spies
at that one restaurant, they order Surf and

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:55.690
Turf.

00:06:55.690 --> 00:06:58.870
This continues the trend of characters ordering
the very thing they are going after.

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:03.090
The Russians want control over an amphibious
creature, meaning he can get around underwater,

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:04.620
or on land.

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:05.620
Surf or turf.

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:09.680
The water may also foreshadow the connection
shared by Elisa and Gabe.

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:12.710
We see early on that she loves the idea of
being underwater.

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:15.900
This could have something to do with the fact
that she’s mute and you can only communicate

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:17.560
in sign under the surface.

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:20.530
But she dreams of living underwater before
she ever meets Gabe.

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:24.190
And early on she’s seen masturbating in
the tub, which is the same place that she

00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:28.120
ends up hiding Gabe, so that could allude
to their sexual relationship.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:30.900
And then the bathroom is the first place that
they…

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:32.340
well you get the picture.

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:35.970
And then finally we have the last quote of
the film delivered by Giles.

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:39.860
“Unable to perceive the shape of you, I
find you all around me.”

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:45.479
He’s talking about water, which as I mentioned
in my last video, has no set molecular structure,

00:07:45.479 --> 00:07:49.800
no shape; but when submerged, the subject
is completely surrounded by it.

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:54.680
The story of the silent princess comes full
circle, beginning and ending with her fully

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:56.289
submerged in water.

00:07:56.289 --> 00:08:01.889
In both the opening and ending scenes, we
see the next major motif, shoes.

00:08:01.889 --> 00:08:06.440
One of Elisa’s shoes can be seen floating
in a closet in the opening dream sequence,

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:10.490
and when she joins Gabe in the ocean at the
end of the journey the shoe is seen slipping

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:11.490
off her foot.

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:15.440
It’s almost as if she’s been dreaming
about a fish person coming into her life and

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:19.130
sweeping her off her feet all along, but that’s
really weird to think about.

00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:22.900
In the last video we talked about the idea
of shoes representing her life on land and

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:24.830
her not needing them in ocean.

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:28.580
So the shoe slipping off her foot could represent
her giving up her life on land.

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:34.250
I’m going to take that theory one step further,
pun intended, and say that Elisa’s shoes

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:39.220
represent her way of artistic expression and
communication, and while living underwater,

00:08:39.220 --> 00:08:40.719
no longer needs them.

00:08:40.719 --> 00:08:45.180
Early on we see her cleaning the shoes to
give that crisp tap sound, so when she’s

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:50.130
prancing down the hall or tap dancing with
Giles in front of the TV she expresses herself

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:51.760
rhythmically, or musically.

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:56.180
She’s obviously interested in music and
singing, as evidenced by her whistling and

00:08:56.180 --> 00:09:00.360
the part where she actually speaks and imagines
herself singing on a stage with Gabe.

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:04.880
We also see her literally using that tapping
sound to communicate, like when she taps the

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:09.350
wall to get Giles attention or when Zelda
asks her to tap the phone so she knows Elisa

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:10.770
is listening.

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:12.350
Whoa, hold up a second.

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:13.350
Time out.

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:16.190
How weird is it that I’ve been using this
Legend of Zelda clip to demonstrate the extended

00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:20.100
lifespan of amphibious fish people, and it
turns out there’s a character in this movie

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:21.170
named Zelda.

00:09:21.170 --> 00:09:25.269
And on top of that, one of the characters
in the movie is referred to as a princess.

00:09:25.269 --> 00:09:28.260
AND on top of that, we have a silent protagonist
who wears green!

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:30.320
Just wanted to point that out.

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:32.870
If you know what I’m talking about, leave
a like on this video.

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:37.430
But anyways, back on topic here, Zelda complains
when her feet are killing her while they are

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:38.430
working.

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:42.550
So if shoes are the thing that grounds you
to the land on the surface and Elisa is ditching

00:09:42.550 --> 00:09:46.370
them to be with her soulmate underwater, then
Zelda’s shoes must represent her marriage

00:09:46.370 --> 00:09:50.190
to her husband, which we later find out is
not a good situation.

00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:51.890
Maybe I’m looking too far into that, I don’t
know.

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:54.430
I’m sure I’ll hear it from you guys if
I am.

00:09:54.430 --> 00:09:58.880
Much of the movie has Gabe locked up, with
Elisa and Zelda trying to get him to freedom.

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:01.500
Both characters have symbols of flying free
on them.

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:05.640
Zelda has this bird pin that she wears during
the breakout scene and there are also some

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:08.060
similar birds on the wall in her home.

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:10.840
Elisa has a butterfly jewel on her collar.

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:14.620
They had this wardrobe display setup at my
theater, so here’s better look.

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:19.000
Take this with a grain of salt, but Strickland
tells Elisa he thinks he can make her squack,

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:21.700
which is possibly another bird reference?

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:26.060
In any event, we can infer that Strickland
has no game… whatsoever.

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:29.150
Gabe on the other hand....

00:10:29.150 --> 00:10:30.150
You know what?

00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:32.110
That will probably never seem normal to me.

00:10:32.110 --> 00:10:34.610
No matter how many times I see this movie.

00:10:34.610 --> 00:10:38.870
One other thing that stood out to me was what
appeared to be an Egyptian hieroglyphics design

00:10:38.870 --> 00:10:40.149
on Giles’s couch.

00:10:40.149 --> 00:10:44.720
The ancient Egyptians are known for building
the Sphinx and actually worshipping cats as

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:45.720
Gods.

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:48.450
You’ll notice that Giles has over a million
cats running around.

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:51.880
He has so many that it’s not even that big
of a deal when Gabe bites one of their heads

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:52.880
off.

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:56.720
That cat by the way, was named Pandora, which
is another reference to Greek mythology.

00:10:56.720 --> 00:11:01.080
According to Strickland, Gabe had been worshipped
by the Amazonians as a God as well, before

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:03.990
he was captured in a river in South America.

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:07.820
And for those of you who are going to ask,
yes, I do still think it’s possible that

00:11:07.820 --> 00:11:11.180
Gabe is unofficially the Creature from the
Black Lagoon.

00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:15.740
The information we got in the movie only furthers
the notion, and when Gabe carries Elisa into

00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:19.769
the water just before the credits rolled,
I immediately got a flashback to the way the

00:11:19.769 --> 00:11:23.930
the Creature picks up Kay and carries her
in Creature from the Black Lagoon.

00:11:23.930 --> 00:11:26.800
It almost had to be a nod from Del Toro.

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:31.720
But my point is that the Amazonians worshipped
Gabe as divinity, and by the end of the movie,

00:11:31.720 --> 00:11:36.570
he makes a case that he really is a God by
healing some injuries John Coffee style, giving

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:40.910
Elisa the power to control water and possibly
causing it to rain so that the canal would

00:11:40.910 --> 00:11:43.190
fill up in time for the floodgates to open.

00:11:43.190 --> 00:11:47.670
He’s also captivated at the movie theater
watching “The Story of Ruth”, which I

00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:51.350
haven’t seen but apparently involves the
worship of Gods and idols.

00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:56.529
Did anyone catch that movie theater was supposedly
“scientifically air conditioned?”

00:11:56.529 --> 00:12:00.029
What does that even mean?

00:12:00.029 --> 00:12:03.339
Anyways, let’s see what we’ve got in here?

00:12:03.339 --> 00:12:06.480
There are a couple of posters in the women’s
locker room at Oceam to analyse.

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:10.160
The first is simple a PSA by the City of Baltimore
to save water.

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:12.930
The other says, “Loose lips might sink ships.”

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:17.639
My first thought was the song XO, by Fall
Out Boy, but given that the movie takes place

00:12:17.639 --> 00:12:22.529
43 years before From Under the Cork Tree came
out, I decided to look it up, and apparently

00:12:22.529 --> 00:12:26.860
it was an actual piece of WWII propaganda,
basically encouraging soldiers not to give

00:12:26.860 --> 00:12:28.560
away their plan to the enemy.

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:33.250
I find the choice kind of ironic, considering
that Elisa has no voice, due to her neck being

00:12:33.250 --> 00:12:34.250
injured as a baby.

00:12:34.250 --> 00:12:37.820
It’s not entirely clear what happens at
the end of the movie, but I got the sense

00:12:37.820 --> 00:12:42.500
that her neck scars ended up being a blessing
in disguise because Gabe turns them into gills.

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:46.620
It seemed to be like she was able to breath
underwater at the end, but let me know how

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:48.110
you perceived it too.

00:12:48.110 --> 00:12:52.760
It was also symbolic how Gabe stabs Strickland
in the throat at the end, so that as Elisa

00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:56.970
moves to a place where she doesn’t need
her voice, Strickland is silenced.

00:12:56.970 --> 00:13:00.610
And if there’s a meaning behind Strickland’s
death, I think his initial injury, where he

00:13:00.610 --> 00:13:03.300
loses his fingers, is also significant.

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:06.430
He ends up losing his ring finger and his
pinky finger.

00:13:06.430 --> 00:13:11.279
